@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.808 = private unnamed_addr constant [34 x i8] c"django-nullable-model-string-field", align 1
@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.809 = private unnamed_addr constant [791 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks nullable string-based fields (like `CharField` and `TextField`)\0Ain Django models.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf a string-based field is nullable, then your model will have two possible\0Arepresentations for \22no data\22: `None` and the empty string. This can lead to\0Aconfusion, as clients of the API have to check for both `None` and the\0Aempty string when trying to determine if the field has data.\0A\0AThe Django convention is to use the empty string in lieu of `None` for\0Astring-based fields.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom django.db import models\0A\0A\0Aclass MyModel(models.Model):\0A    field = models.CharField(max_length=255, null=True)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom django.db import models\0A\0A\0Aclass MyModel(models.Model):\0A    field = models.CharField(max_length=255, default=\22\22)\0A```\0A", align 1
