@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.862 = private unnamed_addr constant [1129 x i8] c"## What it does\0ACheck for `if`-`else`-blocks that can be replaced with a ternary operator.\0AMoreover, in [preview], check if these ternary expressions can be\0Afurther simplified to binary expressions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`if`-`else`-blocks that assign a value to a variable in both branches can\0Abe expressed more concisely by using a ternary or binary operator.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aif foo:\0A    bar = x\0Aelse:\0A    bar = y\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Abar = x if foo else y\0A```\0A\0AOr, in [preview]:\0A\0A```python\0Aif cond:\0A    z = cond\0Aelse:\0A    z = other_cond\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Az = cond or other_cond\0A```\0A\0A## Known issues\0AThis is an opinionated style rule that may not always be to everyone's\0Ataste, especially for code that makes use of complex `if` conditions.\0ATernary operators can also make it harder to measure [code coverage]\0Awith tools that use line profiling.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Conditional expressions](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#conditional-expressions)\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A[code coverage]: https://github.com/nedbat/coveragepy/issues/509\0A", align 1
