@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.912 = private unnamed_addr constant [1178 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `for` loops that can be replaced by a list comprehension.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen creating a transformed list from an existing list using a for-loop,\0Aprefer a list comprehension. List comprehensions are more readable and\0Amore performant.\0A\0AUsing the below as an example, the list comprehension is ~10% faster on\0APython 3.11, and ~25% faster on Python 3.10.\0A\0ANote that, as with all `perflint` rules, this is only intended as a\0Amicro-optimization, and will have a negligible impact on performance in\0Amost cases.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aoriginal = list(range(10000))\0Afiltered = []\0Afor i in original:\0A    if i % 2:\0A        filtered.append(i)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aoriginal = list(range(10000))\0Afiltered = [x for x in original if x % 2]\0A```\0A\0AIf you're appending to an existing list, use the `extend` method instead:\0A```python\0Aoriginal = list(range(10000))\0Afiltered.extend(x for x in original if x % 2)\0A```\0A\0ATake care that if the original for-loop uses an assignment expression\0Aas a conditional, such as `if match:=re.match(\22\\d+\22,\22123\22)`, then\0Athe corresponding comprehension must wrap the assignment\0Aexpression in parentheses to avoid a syntax error.\0A", align 1
