@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.997 = private unnamed_addr constant [1276 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for variables defined in `for` loops and `with` statements that\0Aget overwritten within the body, for example by another `for` loop or\0A`with` statement or by direct assignment.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ARedefinition of a loop variable inside the loop's body causes its value\0Ato differ from the original loop iteration for the remainder of the\0Ablock, in a way that will likely cause bugs.\0A\0AIn Python, unlike many other languages, `for` loops and `with`\0Astatements don't define their own scopes. Therefore, a nested loop that\0Auses the same target variable name as an outer loop will reuse the same\0Aactual variable, and the value from the last iteration will \22leak out\22\0Ainto the remainder of the enclosing loop.\0A\0AWhile this mistake is easy to spot in small examples, it can be hidden\0Ain larger blocks of code, where the definition and redefinition of the\0Avariable may not be visible at the same time.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afor i in range(10):\0A    i = 9\0A    print(i)  # prints 9 every iteration\0A\0Afor i in range(10):\0A    for i in range(10):  # original value overwritten\0A        pass\0A    print(i)  # also prints 9 every iteration\0A\0Awith path1.open() as f:\0A    with path2.open() as f:\0A        f = path2.open()\0A    print(f.readline())  # prints a line from path2\0A```\0A", align 1
